[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the eBGP path between our network and the partner (router1 AS1 <-> router2 AS2) and could not reproduce any instability. The BGP control plane is healthy: on router1 the neighbor 193.10.11.2 is Established (Up/Down 00:02:24) with 1 IPv4 prefix received, and router1’s RIB shows B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0. Symmetrically, router2 has B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. FRR logs on both routers show regular keepalives with no session resets after initial bring-up. Data plane is also clean: sustained low-interval pings (60 probes at 100 ms) from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) and back had 0% loss, and host defaults point correctly to router1/router2. Interfaces on the inter-router link (eth0) show link up with no errors/drops.

Mechanism-wise, if a partner route were flapping, we would see BGP neighbor down/up events or withdrawals in frr.log and route removal/reinstall timestamps in the RIB; none were observed. Localization therefore does not point to router1, router2, or their eth0 link at this time.

Root cause: No current fault found; the eBGP session and learned route to 200.1.1.0/24 are stable, suggesting any past interruption was transient and has cleared.

Corrective fix: No changes required now. To catch any recurrence, enable tighter telemetry (e.g., watch frr.log and “show ip bgp summary” over time), or optionally deploy BFD/shorter keepalives to reduce convergence impact if a future flap occurs. === END ===